edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186475771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186475702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475908 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ffba39de-be3f-50d3-b059-2377c3c0d428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475939 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475939 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475939 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475939 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475939 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63615617-5b80-58c9-8714-0059cfaa1f62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475939 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries of the main diagonal. Therefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=7.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{7}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{7})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{4}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{4})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{7})+\\text{dim}({V}_{4})=1+2=3.\\,✓\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475976 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e91960f2-bae8-5967-8997-6021ee7e8a3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475976 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $D={P}^{-1}AP$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we find that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nThe second column of $P$ corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-\\lambda I,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={x}_{2}-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the second column of $P,$ we deduce that ${x}_{2}=0.$ Now, setting ${x}_{3}=-1$ and substituting our values for ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ into the general solution, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475978 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475978 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475978 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475978 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9c15a3d-862e-553a-bd17-8b80866daf10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475978 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )+4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=2.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{2}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{2})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{5}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-5I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -7 & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). This implies that we will have $3-2=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{5})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is *not* diagonalizable, since \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{2})+\\text{dim}({V}_{5})=1+1=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475980 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186475902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475980 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475980 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475980 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475980 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed2c40c3-1b90-504b-a86e-ac9274a7e945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186475980 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 8 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-8) & =0 \\\\ -(\\lambda -5)({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -20) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-4}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-4})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{5}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-5I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+8{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). This implies that we will have $3-1=2$ linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{5})=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, since\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-4})+\\text{dim}({V}_{5})=1+2=3.\\,✓\n$$\n\nFinally, a corresponding diagonal matrix is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
